<%# components/_overflow_fade — single-line text that fades at its overflowing
    edge (mask-image, mirroring the turf-monster nav) and, when scroll_when is a
    truthy Alpine expression, slides to its end while that holds and snaps back
    when it clears. When the text already fits its box, fade and scroll are both
    no-ops.

    Locals:
      text        (required) the string to render
      class:      (optional) typography / colour classes for the text
      scroll_when (optional) an Alpine expression evaluated in the surrounding
                  scope (e.g. hovered). Omit for a static fade with no scroll. %>
<% text        = local_assigns.fetch(:text) %>
<% text_class  = local_assigns.fetch(:class, "") %>
<% scroll_when = local_assigns.fetch(:scroll_when, "false").to_s.presence || "false" %>
<%# scroll_speed: constant marquee pace in px/sec, so longer text simply takes
    longer (steady to read) instead of racing the same fixed duration. %>
<% scroll_speed = local_assigns.fetch(:scroll_speed, 60).to_i %>
<%# WHY A ResizeObserver AND NOT A RESIZE EVENT. This used to measure once at
    init and then re-measure on a debounced window resize, and nothing else. A
    window resize is one CAUSE of the box changing; the observer watches the
    EFFECT. That distinction is the whole bug: on this app the box mostly moves
    without the window moving at all — an async value painting into a
    neighbouring slot (the category chip beside a heartbeat goal, the claim chip
    on a task card), a font swap, a sibling expanding, a Turbo stream replacing
    the row. None of those announce themselves, and every one of them landed
    after the only measurement anyone had taken. Worse, init() is the moment the
    neighbouring slot is still on its narrow un-hydrated render, so the text has
    the most room it will ever have and looks like it fits.

    The same bug in turf-monster's nav (PR 391) never applied the mask on the
    live path at any width, for exactly this reason.

    BOTH BOXES ARE WATCHED. The container shrinks when a neighbour widens; the
    inner span widens when the font swaps under an unchanged container. Watching
    one misses the other.

    Reading layout in the callback cannot loop: the only things a measurement
    changes are mask-image on the container and transform plus
    transition-duration on the span, all of which paint and none of which
    reflow.

    The observer belongs to the instance and is released in destroy(), so a
    Turbo navigation that re-inits Alpine over restored DOM cannot leave one
    behind. The first measurement waits a tick because x-ref children are not
    wired when a parent init() runs, and _dead cancels that deferred setup if
    the element is torn down inside the tick. %>
<div class="relative overflow-hidden min-w-0"
     data-overflow-fade
     x-data="{
       overflowPx: 0,
       overflows: false,
       speed: <%= scroll_speed %>,
       _ro: null,
       _dead: false,
       fadeRight: 'mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, #000 80%, transparent); -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, #000 80%, transparent)',
       fadeLeft: 'mask-image: linear-gradient(to left, #000 80%, transparent); -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to left, #000 80%, transparent)',
       init() {
         this.$nextTick(() => {
           if (this._dead) return
           this.measure()
           if (!window.ResizeObserver) return
           this._ro = new ResizeObserver(() => this.measure())
           this._ro.observe(this.$el)
           this._ro.observe(this.$refs.fadeInner)
         })
       },
       destroy() {
         this._dead = true
         if (this._ro) { this._ro.disconnect(); this._ro = null }
       },
       measure() {
         this.overflowPx = Math.max(0, this.$refs.fadeInner.scrollWidth - this.$el.clientWidth)
         this.overflows = this.overflowPx > 1
       }
     }"
     :style="!overflows ? '' : ((<%= scroll_when %>) ? fadeLeft : fadeRight)">
  <%# Duration tracks the distance (overflowPx / speed) so the glyphs always travel
      at the same px/sec — a steady read, not a same-time dash to the end. %>
  <span x-ref="fadeInner"
        class="inline-block whitespace-nowrap will-change-transform transition-transform ease-linear <%= text_class %>"
        :style="'transition-duration: ' + Math.round(overflowPx / speed * 1000) + 'ms; transform: translateX(' + ((overflows && (<%= scroll_when %>)) ? -overflowPx : 0) + 'px)'">
    <%= text %>
  </span>
</div>
